        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "A real channel changes what arrives. Thermal noise spreads each ideal point into a cloud. Frequency or phase offset rotates the constellation. Fading changes amplitude, and multipath can smear one symbol into the next. These effects are different and require different corrections. A receiver chain may estimate timing, carrier phase, channel gain, and equalization before making bit decisions. Looking only at a waveform can hide the pattern; the constellation exposes whether uncertainty is random spread, systematic rotation, scaling, or intersymbol interference."
        },
