        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "Each constellation point supplies two coordinates: I for the cosine carrier and Q for the sine carrier. The carriers are ninety degrees apart, so they are orthogonal over the symbol interval. We weight them by the chosen coordinates and add them to create one real passband waveform. Changing the bit pair changes the carrier phase while maintaining a similar envelope. Orthogonality lets the receiver later separate the two components, provided timing and carrier reference are sufficiently aligned."
