        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "Quadrature phase-shift keying, or QPSK, carries two bits in each symbol. We group the incoming stream into pairs and map each pair to one of four points in the in-phase and quadrature plane. Neighboring points are often assigned bit patterns that differ by one bit, a Gray mapping that reduces the number of bit errors when noise pushes a symbol across one nearby boundary. The constellation is more than a diagram: it is the transmitter's vocabulary and the receiver's decision map."
        },
