        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "After synchronization and matched filtering, the receiver samples once per symbol and selects the closest valid point. In basic QPSK, the I and Q signs define four decision regions. A sample near a boundary is fragile because a small noise change can flip the decision. Increasing signal energy separates the clusters relative to noise, reducing error probability, but power and bandwidth are limited resources. Receiver design therefore balances reliable decisions against spectral efficiency, energy, and implementation complexity."
        },
